    "updated_at": "2024-03-27T10:55:47Z",
    "body": "Here's a test-case. One quirk here is that at least Blink and Gecko parse `*:hover` and `:hover` to exactly the same thing. So ignoring that `*` special-case, yes, I think the chromium behavior is as I described above (for simple selectors at least). Not sure what the `is_sub_selector` in chromium conveys, it seems a mix of \"being used for pseudo-elements\" and \"being in a nested pseudo-class / slotted / etc thing\".\r\n\r\n```html\r\n<!doctype html>\r\n<body>\r\n<script>\r\n  let selectors = [\r\n    `*:hover`,\r\n    `:hover`,\r\n    `span:hover`,\r\n    `:is(a, span):hover`,\r\n    `:is(span):hover`,\r\n    `:not(div):hover`,\r\n    `[data-attr]:hover`,\r\n    `:hover:nth-child(1)`,\r\n    `:hover:first-child`,\r\n  ];\r\n\r\n  for (let selector of selectors) {\r\n    let frame = document.createElement(\"iframe\");\r\n    frame.src = `data:text/html,` + encodeURIComponent(`\r\n      <style>${selector} { background-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, .2) }</style>\r\n      <div id=root>\r\n        <p><strong>${selector}</strong>\r\n        <p><a data-attr href=\"#\">Link</a>\r\n        <p><span data-attr>Non-link</span>\r\n      </div>\r\n    `);\r\n    document.body.appendChild(frame);\r\n  }\r\n</script>\r\n```",
